[[:db/retract 17592186134352 :question/answer-fields 17592186132151] [:db/retract 17592186134326 :question/answer-fields 17592186131995] [:db/retract 17592186134324 :question/answer-fields 17592186131983] {:db/id 17592186133864 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5b19eecb-3ecc-5584-a617-4bf8675e0b01" "question-a6266ee7-76af-56d5-9957-fa402398ddd2" "question-71004f41-ae2f-50fd-9fdc-88ec92c18337"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133680 :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $3 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix\n\nII. The columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\nIII. $R$ is an upper triangular matrix" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133862 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e1307433-5f5d-5677-8396-c15cc88708bf" "question-c68cf512-7931-57e9-a96b-53e13136a3ce"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133678 :question/problem "![](images/db/db0726bd1986ffe1876c4b5a93b7b14b4f4c1496dc6b28ebd6ac002ea878cde2.svg)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3a08d141-4335-56a2-8562-7ce4a6f3683b" "question-e5f7e59e-edf4-549f-aea0-27ed8989142e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133633 :question/problem "![](images/db/db19bfb8029513dcb3dc109a0752f87ecf405a6aa56881849f553d30fa1085ff.svg)\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\big\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_1\\Vert =2,$ $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_2\\Vert =3,$ $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}_3\\Vert =4,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-3{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $r_{13}+r_{23}+r_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2,\\mathbf{v}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}=2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=3{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{4}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2,\\mathbf{q}_3 \\big\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/f5/f53a5eb3b2d24ad186382f8eed42775b2ee61d34a20a0af821a38c17d846e873.svg)\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n![](images/27/2797cd9e375b3a28f3f8afc8c49b85e202ad5cc87fb5a332ebb9abd73f388ee3.svg)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nr_{13}+r_{23}+r_{33} = 6+(-6)+4 = 4.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-946d71c0-c7c2-59d9-aaa7-5ecb48201289"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133679 :question/problem "![](images/12/128c3de38ca9c80e3e0d22d939fe05c88404fed97dee1a94f59aff5331f56172.svg)\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns $\\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of $q_{12} \\cdot r_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct a matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2\\mathbf{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by $Q^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/5d/5d8b0271c6dcd970ec7c8d652433a798daf97e67792c47db1f6eef4bd39ad651.svg)\n\nFinally, $q_{12} \\cdot r_{22} = -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\cdot 3 = -2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133798 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8825d6b3-93be-5a03-9cc7-5000a40034aa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133607 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d29d0f5dd5e0d2c79c8d2c40c49b358a01dc5bb5536153fe05068773ef1e228.svg)\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns $\\mathbf{q}_1,\\mathbf{q}_2$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of $q_{11} \\cdot r_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ![](images/71/714ad80d47ea58159e0e486f5bfc5a6657fd441bc47aa2b8fa7230a89bdb0445.svg)\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{12}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/a6/a6377dfb1e7ed7afafe348354dd21b2195c12495d9a5a6dcd98fec3aaeb8162d.svg)\n\nFinally, $q_{11} \\cdot r_{12} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right) \\cdot \\left({2\\sqrt6}\\right) = 2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186363203 :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $5\\times 4$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The columns of $Q$ are orthogonal\n\nII. $Q$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix\n\nIII. $R$ is a  square  matrix" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-5b19eecb-3ecc-5584-a617-4bf8675e0b01" :question/id #uuid "5b19eecb-3ecc-5584-a617-4bf8675e0b01" :question/math-academy-id "q-95917" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $4\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is a $4\\times 2$ upper triangular matrix\n\nII. The columns of $Q$ are unit vectors\n\nIII. $R$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be a $4\\times 2$ matrix, but is not necessarily upper triangular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis, they are unit vectors.\n\n- Statement III is false. $R$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-77a7384a-2954-50d8-86d9-02fc2c11bedd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-77a7384a-2954-50d8-86d9-02fc2c11bedd" :answer-field/id #uuid "77a7384a-2954-50d8-86d9-02fc2c11bedd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7bc9001c-a8a7-5655-ad38-e28393932a25" :answer-field/choices ["answer-60ddcb4a-b5db-50a3-82d4-5c076834cb99" "answer-40511cf5-4e38-545c-92bd-6f12dbd5bbe6" "answer-429b9536-a311-5367-b367-ede0065e5d2d" "answer-7bc9001c-a8a7-5655-ad38-e28393932a25" "answer-0855b0c5-d3cb-5d5a-87d3-6ae2573808c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60ddcb4a-b5db-50a3-82d4-5c076834cb99" :answer/id #uuid "60ddcb4a-b5db-50a3-82d4-5c076834cb99" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40511cf5-4e38-545c-92bd-6f12dbd5bbe6" :answer/id #uuid "40511cf5-4e38-545c-92bd-6f12dbd5bbe6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-429b9536-a311-5367-b367-ede0065e5d2d" :answer/id #uuid "429b9536-a311-5367-b367-ede0065e5d2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bc9001c-a8a7-5655-ad38-e28393932a25" :answer/id #uuid "7bc9001c-a8a7-5655-ad38-e28393932a25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0855b0c5-d3cb-5d5a-87d3-6ae2573808c2" :answer/id #uuid "0855b0c5-d3cb-5d5a-87d3-6ae2573808c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a6266ee7-76af-56d5-9957-fa402398ddd2" :question/id #uuid "a6266ee7-76af-56d5-9957-fa402398ddd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95923" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $8\\times 4$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is an $8\\times 4$ matrix\n\nII. $R$ is an $8\\times 8$ matrix\n\nIII. $R$ is an upper triangular matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a  QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $Q$ must be an $8\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. $R$ should be a $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed $R$ must be an upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-16d9df2f-7dae-571c-9898-8b25f88afb03"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-16d9df2f-7dae-571c-9898-8b25f88afb03" :answer-field/id #uuid "16d9df2f-7dae-571c-9898-8b25f88afb03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ee9fd3db-d080-510c-8cba-0c10cffe8599" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f0b12155-382a-5236-96d0-5241fdc65d8e" "answer-ee9fd3db-d080-510c-8cba-0c10cffe8599" "answer-c4b65933-5b0f-5c15-a729-3b530d4943b2" "answer-b3c6518b-cfa4-5881-b743-5099d5ac42c2" "answer-34dee133-5da2-587d-bf0e-88da035eca52"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0b12155-382a-5236-96d0-5241fdc65d8e" :answer/id #uuid "f0b12155-382a-5236-96d0-5241fdc65d8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee9fd3db-d080-510c-8cba-0c10cffe8599" :answer/id #uuid "ee9fd3db-d080-510c-8cba-0c10cffe8599" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4b65933-5b0f-5c15-a729-3b530d4943b2" :answer/id #uuid "c4b65933-5b0f-5c15-a729-3b530d4943b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3c6518b-cfa4-5881-b743-5099d5ac42c2" :answer/id #uuid "b3c6518b-cfa4-5881-b743-5099d5ac42c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34dee133-5da2-587d-bf0e-88da035eca52" :answer/id #uuid "34dee133-5da2-587d-bf0e-88da035eca52" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186364571 :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The columns of $Q$ are orthonormal\n\nII. $Q$ is a square matrix\n\nIII. $R$ is a square matrix" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-71004f41-ae2f-50fd-9fdc-88ec92c18337" :question/id #uuid "71004f41-ae2f-50fd-9fdc-88ec92c18337" :question/math-academy-id "q-95919" :question/problem "Given that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of a $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)$ is spanned by the columns of $Q$\n\nIII. $R$ is a square matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be an $m\\times n$ matrix, so $Q$ is not necessarily a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Since the columns of $Q$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A),$ they must span $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be an $m\\times n$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7fd0a920-47da-5738-85dc-fcfde1071d1a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7fd0a920-47da-5738-85dc-fcfde1071d1a" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd0a920-47da-5738-85dc-fcfde1071d1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3108f868-6810-5414-b79d-ed9c59cf14cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-30cd0132-8138-5a90-bf6d-84623e148b9a" "answer-1194f3e3-1844-5ab9-9daf-5533b0a96a9d" "answer-62bcf903-0255-59aa-affe-9d6ade55eb53" "answer-3108f868-6810-5414-b79d-ed9c59cf14cc" "answer-eede4f00-fcca-5952-bf0b-1b45f39cc595"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30cd0132-8138-5a90-bf6d-84623e148b9a" :answer/id #uuid "30cd0132-8138-5a90-bf6d-84623e148b9a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1194f3e3-1844-5ab9-9daf-5533b0a96a9d" :answer/id #uuid "1194f3e3-1844-5ab9-9daf-5533b0a96a9d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62bcf903-0255-59aa-affe-9d6ade55eb53" :answer/id #uuid "62bcf903-0255-59aa-affe-9d6ade55eb53" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3108f868-6810-5414-b79d-ed9c59cf14cc" :answer/id #uuid "3108f868-6810-5414-b79d-ed9c59cf14cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eede4f00-fcca-5952-bf0b-1b45f39cc595" :answer/id #uuid "eede4f00-fcca-5952-bf0b-1b45f39cc595" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e1307433-5f5d-5677-8396-c15cc88708bf" :question/id #uuid "e1307433-5f5d-5677-8396-c15cc88708bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95924" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-34f73934-6b3e-5362-85fb-c078d25b34ac"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-34f73934-6b3e-5362-85fb-c078d25b34ac" :answer-field/id #uuid "34f73934-6b3e-5362-85fb-c078d25b34ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6759f5b6-d249-5365-af63-b9e06fadd6c6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b8e8a3d2-47ca-598e-8298-401f66b07744" "answer-965ab4c3-b74e-52d8-a355-600cbb8426c0" "answer-6759f5b6-d249-5365-af63-b9e06fadd6c6" "answer-5bd1aa0c-d1f1-5e19-8c7c-5cd02697146c" "answer-8130fec1-ce90-56ae-8304-b2a9297e4bd1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8e8a3d2-47ca-598e-8298-401f66b07744" :answer/id #uuid "b8e8a3d2-47ca-598e-8298-401f66b07744" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-965ab4c3-b74e-52d8-a355-600cbb8426c0" :answer/id #uuid "965ab4c3-b74e-52d8-a355-600cbb8426c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6759f5b6-d249-5365-af63-b9e06fadd6c6" :answer/id #uuid "6759f5b6-d249-5365-af63-b9e06fadd6c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bd1aa0c-d1f1-5e19-8c7c-5cd02697146c" :answer/id #uuid "5bd1aa0c-d1f1-5e19-8c7c-5cd02697146c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8130fec1-ce90-56ae-8304-b2a9297e4bd1" :answer/id #uuid "8130fec1-ce90-56ae-8304-b2a9297e4bd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c68cf512-7931-57e9-a96b-53e13136a3ce" :question/id #uuid "c68cf512-7931-57e9-a96b-53e13136a3ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-95933" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-07052409-2507-5fcc-b1d5-dc865a195f43"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-07052409-2507-5fcc-b1d5-dc865a195f43" :answer-field/id #uuid "07052409-2507-5fcc-b1d5-dc865a195f43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-45e9c0e6-c88e-53a1-9c43-cbdad38b1c50" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a92bde20-f3b2-50bb-885a-b59500259f0d" "answer-5e041f60-e112-5cdc-8ca9-0a76aca85f33" "answer-3b22f01e-7a4d-5708-9e4c-f98dc75ccfb4" "answer-45e9c0e6-c88e-53a1-9c43-cbdad38b1c50" "answer-f7fb81ed-14f8-585c-8ccb-5a4e29de6310"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a92bde20-f3b2-50bb-885a-b59500259f0d" :answer/id #uuid "a92bde20-f3b2-50bb-885a-b59500259f0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e041f60-e112-5cdc-8ca9-0a76aca85f33" :answer/id #uuid "5e041f60-e112-5cdc-8ca9-0a76aca85f33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b22f01e-7a4d-5708-9e4c-f98dc75ccfb4" :answer/id #uuid "3b22f01e-7a4d-5708-9e4c-f98dc75ccfb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45e9c0e6-c88e-53a1-9c43-cbdad38b1c50" :answer/id #uuid "45e9c0e6-c88e-53a1-9c43-cbdad38b1c50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7fb81ed-14f8-585c-8ccb-5a4e29de6310" :answer/id #uuid "f7fb81ed-14f8-585c-8ccb-5a4e29de6310" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3a08d141-4335-56a2-8562-7ce4a6f3683b" :question/id #uuid "3a08d141-4335-56a2-8562-7ce4a6f3683b" :question/math-academy-id "q-41484" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{2}+6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{2}+6{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-30{q}_{1}+5{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=8{q}_{1}-4{q}_{2}+4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -30 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{23}=3+8+(-4)=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bcc7880c-e1d9-51f3-9d82-55e1eee95989"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bcc7880c-e1d9-51f3-9d82-55e1eee95989" :answer-field/id #uuid "bcc7880c-e1d9-51f3-9d82-55e1eee95989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cfb5e98f-f826-54b9-8c31-84bb424125fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70fe47a0-cdd1-5db7-baec-800ac5639ef7" "answer-49b4d1d7-f1c4-5298-a995-47dd049adbd4" "answer-061380b4-8fa4-5c94-aa1d-0aa7eefd118f" "answer-cfb5e98f-f826-54b9-8c31-84bb424125fc" "answer-003af35e-f600-5607-8b84-03a3fd662edc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70fe47a0-cdd1-5db7-baec-800ac5639ef7" :answer/id #uuid "70fe47a0-cdd1-5db7-baec-800ac5639ef7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49b4d1d7-f1c4-5298-a995-47dd049adbd4" :answer/id #uuid "49b4d1d7-f1c4-5298-a995-47dd049adbd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-061380b4-8fa4-5c94-aa1d-0aa7eefd118f" :answer/id #uuid "061380b4-8fa4-5c94-aa1d-0aa7eefd118f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfb5e98f-f826-54b9-8c31-84bb424125fc" :answer/id #uuid "cfb5e98f-f826-54b9-8c31-84bb424125fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-003af35e-f600-5607-8b84-03a3fd662edc" :answer/id #uuid "003af35e-f600-5607-8b84-03a3fd662edc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186364178 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+8{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}-3{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+8{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}-3{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=6{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-16{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=9{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 & -16 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{33}=9+2+3=14.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-e5f7e59e-edf4-549f-aea0-27ed8989142e" :question/id #uuid "e5f7e59e-edf4-549f-aea0-27ed8989142e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140901" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & \\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above, where $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set of vectors. You're given that $‖{v}_{1}‖=1,$ $‖{v}_{2}‖=2,$ $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ and that the system of equations below describes the relationship between the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we normalize the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{1}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{2}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{2}=2{q}_{2} \\\\ {q}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{3}\\,⟹\\,{v}_{3}=3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the set $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}\\}$ will be both orthogonal and normalized.\n\nNext, let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}={a}_{2}-{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{3}={a}_{3}-2{v}_{2}+{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}={v}_{1}={q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}={v}_{1}+{v}_{2}={q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=-{v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}=-{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=1+(-1)+3=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ed11c1c1-5852-58e2-a3f1-e49b2bb10b4b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ed11c1c1-5852-58e2-a3f1-e49b2bb10b4b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed11c1c1-5852-58e2-a3f1-e49b2bb10b4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-040cb4ad-6adb-5b45-a356-8c24b8b87c5f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e88e24de-26c6-5e79-8da5-47c1a175c8d5" "answer-040cb4ad-6adb-5b45-a356-8c24b8b87c5f" "answer-03f77c16-15f2-5876-91ea-1f42c2f49146" "answer-6d46eee3-4e02-5798-955c-810b16c0b207" "answer-589b7138-e564-53cf-b058-e2dd1efd855b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e88e24de-26c6-5e79-8da5-47c1a175c8d5" :answer/id #uuid "e88e24de-26c6-5e79-8da5-47c1a175c8d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-040cb4ad-6adb-5b45-a356-8c24b8b87c5f" :answer/id #uuid "040cb4ad-6adb-5b45-a356-8c24b8b87c5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03f77c16-15f2-5876-91ea-1f42c2f49146" :answer/id #uuid "03f77c16-15f2-5876-91ea-1f42c2f49146" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d46eee3-4e02-5798-955c-810b16c0b207" :answer/id #uuid "6d46eee3-4e02-5798-955c-810b16c0b207" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-589b7138-e564-53cf-b058-e2dd1efd855b" :answer/id #uuid "589b7138-e564-53cf-b058-e2dd1efd855b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186363217 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}+2{q}_{1}-{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{3}+2{q}_{1}-{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-2{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=-6{q}_{1}+3{q}_{2}+3{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{13}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=-6+2+3=-1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134352 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{2}+2{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{3}-4{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=2{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=-8{q}_{1}+4{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=24{q}_{1}-6{q}_{2}+6{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 & 24 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{12}+{r}_{22}+{r}_{23}=-8+4+(-6)=-10.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134356 :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 0=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186132175 :answer-field/correct 17592186132179 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-946d71c0-c7c2-59d9-aaa7-5ecb48201289" :question/id #uuid "946d71c0-c7c2-59d9-aaa7-5ecb48201289" :question/math-academy-id "q-96015" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-039c8e12-c757-5b90-a48b-67f394056d16"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-039c8e12-c757-5b90-a48b-67f394056d16" :answer-field/id #uuid "039c8e12-c757-5b90-a48b-67f394056d16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-988c19f4-4b4b-5658-b6a9-5508c8ebffd9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a409649e-ea5b-5f5d-997d-61959f000b9a" "answer-4edcb67b-0b50-5e1a-880f-c692e26158f7" "answer-988c19f4-4b4b-5658-b6a9-5508c8ebffd9" "answer-4ed122b1-a8ef-54bc-a477-491273558134" "answer-4900a2e7-4ec1-5045-a690-39e44e6c099b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a409649e-ea5b-5f5d-997d-61959f000b9a" :answer/id #uuid "a409649e-ea5b-5f5d-997d-61959f000b9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4edcb67b-0b50-5e1a-880f-c692e26158f7" :answer/id #uuid "4edcb67b-0b50-5e1a-880f-c692e26158f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-988c19f4-4b4b-5658-b6a9-5508c8ebffd9" :answer/id #uuid "988c19f4-4b4b-5658-b6a9-5508c8ebffd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ed122b1-a8ef-54bc-a477-491273558134" :answer/id #uuid "4ed122b1-a8ef-54bc-a477-491273558134" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4900a2e7-4ec1-5045-a690-39e44e6c099b" :answer/id #uuid "4900a2e7-4ec1-5045-a690-39e44e6c099b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186388792 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-(\\frac{4}{2})[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134326 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{9}{9})[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186395107 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{9}{9})[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (-3)=-2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134324 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{12}{6})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & -2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{12}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (-2\\sqrt{6})=2\\sqrt{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-8825d6b3-93be-5a03-9cc7-5000a40034aa" :question/id #uuid "8825d6b3-93be-5a03-9cc7-5000a40034aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-41421" :question/problem "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{11}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{9}{9})[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{11}=(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})\\cdot (3)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=2\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7ebc4500-ebb6-58bb-9b74-0082d64a7b1e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7ebc4500-ebb6-58bb-9b74-0082d64a7b1e" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ebc4500-ebb6-58bb-9b74-0082d64a7b1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8dbb6cf7-0fa9-5dca-bc3c-da7fadfea2d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a86a94a2-9208-5170-9e77-a036ff3be9fc" "answer-39f67c12-f06d-5443-9c63-0e245edcb8a5" "answer-6baa4982-1a24-5642-9737-70e53742cc5e" "answer-8dbb6cf7-0fa9-5dca-bc3c-da7fadfea2d6" "answer-d63bd3dc-77da-5863-8a02-ddc20391b233"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a86a94a2-9208-5170-9e77-a036ff3be9fc" :answer/id #uuid "a86a94a2-9208-5170-9e77-a036ff3be9fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39f67c12-f06d-5443-9c63-0e245edcb8a5" :answer/id #uuid "39f67c12-f06d-5443-9c63-0e245edcb8a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6baa4982-1a24-5642-9737-70e53742cc5e" :answer/id #uuid "6baa4982-1a24-5642-9737-70e53742cc5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dbb6cf7-0fa9-5dca-bc3c-da7fadfea2d6" :answer/id #uuid "8dbb6cf7-0fa9-5dca-bc3c-da7fadfea2d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d63bd3dc-77da-5863-8a02-ddc20391b233" :answer/id #uuid "d63bd3dc-77da-5863-8a02-ddc20391b233" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135058 :tutorial/content "The **QR factorization** of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns is the matrix product\n\n$$\n\\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{A}\n=\n\\underset{{\\large m} \\times {\\large n}}{Q} \\times \\underset{{\\large n} \\times {\\large n}}{R},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m \\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A),$ and\n\n- $R$ is an $n \\times n$ upper triangular matrix with positive entries on its diagonal.\n\nFor example, the $3\\times 2$ matrix\n\n![](images/b7/b71bb9b1dc9aa6d23f2c990052e2e83eeda6000fc64a61ccdb614669d840d38a.svg)\n\nhas the QR factorization\n\n![](images/7c/7c5c30e4011efe0ac87f02217dd1f52c6e64658c6e90d22ad8689209badeb1f4.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135097 :tutorial/content "Given the matrix $Q$ in the QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ how do we find $R?$\n\nRecall that $Q$ has orthonormal columns, so pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by $Q^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}A & ={Q}^{T}QR.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, note that since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal we have $Q^TQ = I_n.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}A & =({Q}^{T}Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}A & ={I}_{n}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can find $R$ by pre-multiplying $A$ by the transpose of $Q.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135098 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown below:\n\n![](images/ee/ee731d95c111101559ad135429198dd7e0b14313272f0933b1c706006da2fbd2.svg)\n\nSuppose also that we are given the following system of equations that connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{2}+3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-2{q}_{1}+{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHow do we find $R?$\nFirst, let's rewrite the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\mathbf{q}_3 \\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/82/824c39c97bb115768856d672b39be8f65d8b4ecbc2ee5d6ccc9f4943594cbacd.svg)\n\nIn this new system, the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_1$ correspond to the first-row entries of $R,$ the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_2$ correspond to the second-row entries of $R,$ and the coefficients of $\\mathbf{q}_3$ correspond to the third-row entries of $R.$ Therefore,\n\n![](images/5b/5ba9887a4d9b47963af171e9275319a2564eca5a4ef8b3ef44978e2ff4b111d9.svg)\n\nTo see why this is, we can take the transpose of both sides of $A=QR{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =QR \\\\ {A}^{T} & =(QR)^{T} \\\\ {A}^{T} & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T} \\\\ \\overset{{A}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {a}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{{R}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{1} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{2} & \\,\\,\\,\\,- \\\\ -\\,\\,\\,\\, & {q}_{3} & \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we were to expand this matrix product, we would obtain our system of equations." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135099 :tutorial/content "Let's now go through the QR factorization algorithm to find the factorization of the matrix\n\n![](images/f0/f0d468f6bce43491b3f97d10185f4393ae8e64198e24bb7708ad66ec57c73df4.svg)\n\n**Step 1**. Orthogonalize the columns of $A$ to get the columns of $Q.$ Using the Gram-Schmidt process, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**. Compute $R=Q^TA.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the QR factorization of $A$ is\n\n![](images/7f/7f0face80ec3823f73e958fae5b78b12641204a8858bb2c8a7677be9827b919d.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134008 :activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
